::JOUR_U03_L08_UA_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.NEWS.01] Source: The student newspaper is preparing a package about a new school recycling initiative. Source notes show that labeled bins will be placed near the cafeteria, gym, and library. The environmental club adviser says the goal is to reduce contamination, which happens when non-recyclable items are mixed with recyclable materials. A student volunteer says concise signs will help students sort items quickly. The editorial board is considering an opinion piece arguing that the school should also add a short recycling reminder to morning announcements. The package needs a factual news brief, a feature angle, an editorial claim with evidence and rebuttal, clear revision, publication conventions, vocabulary support, and a short oral briefing. Question 1: Which response best matches the Unit 3 task? {
=A. Apply the correct Unit 3 journalism skill while preserving source meaning, audience clarity, style, and publication readiness.#Correct. Step 1: identify the Unit 3 journalism skill in the standard. Step 2: compare the answer with the embedded source and lesson model. This answer works because it protects accuracy, audience clarity, organization, and publication readiness.
~B. Use one dramatic opinion sentence for every journalism task.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
~C. Ignore the source and choose the most interesting wording.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
~D. Add unsupported details so the package feels complete.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the exact journalism task. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This choice either adds unsupported information, weakens organization, misuses tone, skips evidence, or fails to communicate clearly to the audience.
}
